-- The opening words of rule 1: every definition below gives way to the context.  As in the keel's
-- Order 1, Rule 3(1), this is one input here and is not threaded through the other functions.
-- src:4197 | 1. In this Order, unless the context otherwise requires — “authorised user” means a person who is designated as an authorised user under Rule 6;
@ref O 28 r 1 (opening words) (src:4197)
GIVEN `the context otherwise requires` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 28 r 1 a defined term has its defined meaning` MEANS
    IF NOT `the context otherwise requires`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "In this Order, unless the context otherwise requires"

-- src:4197 | 1. In this Order, unless the context otherwise requires — “authorised user” means a person who is designated as an authorised user under Rule 6;
@ref O 28 r 1 “authorised user” (src:4197)
GIVEN `the person is designated as an authorised user under Rule 6` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 28 r 1 a person is an authorised user` MEANS
    IF `the person is designated as an authorised user under Rule 6`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“authorised user” means a person who is designated as an authorised user under Rule 6"

-- “deemed” means deemed until the contrary is proved.  The helper every deeming function calls.
-- Before the contrary is proved the deeming stands (JUST TRUE); once it is, the deeming ends (JUST FALSE).
-- This reads "until" as the bar of the ruling of 2026-11-05 (d); see the fork register.
-- src:4198 | “deemed” means deemed until the contrary is proved;
@ref O 28 r 1 “deemed” (src:4198)
GIVEN `the contrary is proved` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 28 r 1 a deeming under this Order stands` MEANS
    IF `the contrary is proved`
    THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“deemed” means deemed until the contrary is proved"
    ELSE JUST TRUE

